<DocsLayout
  title="Cloud Machines"
  eyebrow="Desktop"
  description="Configure a runner execution profile for new desktop threads, with explicit workspace ownership, credentials, and platform limits."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/persistent-agents/", title: "Persistent agents" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/app-server/", title: "App server" }}
>
  <p>
    Cloud Machines are named execution profiles in Roder Desktop. A profile selects
    the runner and workspace used by a new thread's native coding tools.
    The feature is available in the v0.1.9 release line; this guide describes
    <strong>Desktop v0.1.11</strong> with its pinned <strong>Roder v0.4.1</strong> harness.
    It is not the <a href="/docs/roder-cloud/">roder.cloud inference provider</a>.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    Cloud Machines are unavailable on Windows in this desktop version because the
    embedded runner validates remote POSIX paths using host path rules. Use
    <strong>Local machine</strong> for native Windows tools. Existing saved profiles
    remain visible but cannot be selected or run on Windows.
  </div>

  <h2>Prepare the execution workspace</h2>
  <p>
    Prepare the remote directory and repository before starting work. A profile does
    not upload or synchronize your local repository. Choose an absolute workspace
    path on the runner, not a path that only exists on your laptop.
  </p>
  <ol>
    <li>Set the provider's required authentication environment variables before starting Roder Desktop.</li>
    <li>Open <strong>Settings &gt; Cloud Machines</strong> and add a named profile.</li>
    <li>Choose an available runner provider and enter its absolute workspace path.</li>
    <li>Enter only non-secret JSON configuration. Use the provider help below the selector for examples and authentication variables.</li>
    <li>On a new thread, choose the profile in the execution-machine selector beside the policy controls.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    Adding a profile checks its configuration shape and provider availability. It
    does not provision infrastructure or verify credentials. The harness validates
    the runner binding at thread creation, then creates or resumes a runner session
    when a native coding tool first needs it.
  </p>

  <h2>Understand what runs where</h2>
  <p>
    The default is <strong>Local machine</strong>, preserving the native host tools,
    shell, and workspace behavior. Choosing a profile fixes that thread's runner
    binding when the thread is created and persists it in thread metadata.
    Reopening the thread keeps the binding. Changing the new-thread selector does
    not move an existing thread.
  </p>
  <p>
    The local project remains the thread's desktop workspace context. Native coding
    tools use the runner workspace, but the desktop's file panel, terminal, browser,
    and host-provided tools remain desktop-side. This is not a remote desktop or
    a second app-server connection. Do not assume a file visible in a local panel
    exists on the execution machine.
  </p>
  <p>
    Selecting a desktop profile does not change the harness's global runner
    configuration. If a global runner destination is configured, a request for
    Local machine fails instead of silently inheriting the remote destination.
    Clear the global runner setting and restart the app, or select a cloud profile.
  </p>

  <h2>Choose a supported provider</h2>
  <p>
    Desktop discovers available providers through <code>runners/list</code>. The
    pinned harness includes Sprites, Blaxel, Docker, unix-local, Cloudflare, Daytona,
    E2B, Modal, Runloop, and Vercel. The list is a runtime capability catalog rather
    than a guarantee that every vendor's ordinary API can be used directly.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Sprites and Blaxel:</strong> profiles can name an existing machine or sandbox.</li>
    <li><strong>Docker:</strong> requires Docker on the app-server host.</li>
    <li><strong>unix-local:</strong> executes on the app-server host, despite appearing in the runner catalog.</li>
    <li><strong>Cloudflare, Daytona, E2B, Modal, Runloop, and Vercel:</strong> require a compatible runner-gateway <code>/sessions</code> endpoint. Their <code>base_url</code> must not point at an ordinary vendor API.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    This harness version has no SSH runner. An arbitrary SSH hostname is not a
    supported profile. See the <a href="/docs/blaxel-runner/">Blaxel runner guide</a>
    for its harness-level execution contract.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep credentials out of profiles</h2>
  <p>
    Authentication comes from the environment inherited by the desktop app's
    app-server process. Restart the app after changing those variables. Credentials
    are shared per provider within that process; this version has no per-profile
    credential entry or isolated app-server process for each profile.
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    Do not put API keys, passwords, tokens, or credential-bearing URLs in profile
    JSON. Runner configuration is persisted with threads. Desktop rejects
    credential-like fields, but that check cannot recognize every possible secret.
  </div>
  <p>
    Saved profiles use Electron <code>safeStorage</code> encryption in
    <code>&lt;userData&gt;/cloud-machines.enc</code>. Storage fails closed if OS-backed
    encryption is unavailable. The renderer persists only the selected profile ID.
    Invalid or deleted selections fail instead of silently running the prompt locally.
  </p>

  <h2>Remove or change a profile safely</h2>
  <p>
    Removing a profile does not stop or delete cloud resources, change existing
    threads, or transfer their files. Manage the provider's infrastructure separately.
    Start a new thread when you need a different execution binding, and prepare its
    workspace before continuing work.
  </p>

  <h2>Source reference</h2>
  <p>
    These limits and ownership rules follow the tagged
    <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder-desktop/blob/v0.1.11/docs/cloud-machines.md">desktop Cloud Machines contract</a>
    and <a href="https://github.com/RoderAI/roder-desktop/blob/v0.1.11/roder-distro-config.toml">embedded harness pin</a>.
    See <a href="/docs/app-server/">app server</a> for remote-controller integration,
    which is separate from runner execution profiles.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
